<p>A <strong>relational database</strong> stores facts in tables and connects them through shared values. It answers questions across those tables, refuses changes that break specified rules and can make several changes succeed or fail together. Those abilities are useful when several people or processes depend on the same facts.</p>
<p>Consider a shop. Mina orders a mug and two packets of tea. The shop needs to remember who ordered, what each item cost, how much stock remains and whether payment succeeded. Looking up an order is one job. Keeping those facts correct while two people buy the last mug is another.</p>
<p>We will follow the shop from its first tables to the decisions involved in running it. PostgreSQL supplies the concrete SQL and transaction behaviour; other relational engines share the broad ideas but differ in details that matter. Each section ends with a link to a longer article on its subject. Each of those articles works through one complete example first, then links to shorter articles on single questions. The <a href="#choose-a-system-by-testing-the-contract-you-need">reading routes at the end</a> suggest where to start.</p>

<nav aria-label="In this article" class="article-contents"><ol>{sections.map(([id, title]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>

<h2 id="one-action-changes-several-facts">One order, several changes</h2>
<p>Suppose there is one mug left. Accepting a new order must record the order, record its mug item and reduce available stock from one to zero, which we will call reserving the mug. If the application saves those changes separately and stops after the second, the order has an item but the mug is still available to sell. Saving them in a different order only changes which half-finished state is left behind.</p>
<p>The database gives these shared facts one place to be read and changed. All the shop’s application instances use it, and it coordinates their competing writes. A <strong>transaction</strong> lets the application group the three changes so that their committed effect is all or nothing.</p>
<p>The shop still has to say what is correct. “Available stock cannot be negative” is a rule about one stored value. “Every accepted order has reserved its items” also depends on how orders and stock change together. The database can check rules we express; it cannot infer this second rule from a table called <code>orders</code>.</p>
<p>Payment belongs to a separate system. The shop can store a payment attempt, but rolling back its database cannot undo a charge at the provider. Each order needs a status that records where it stands, such as waiting for payment (stored as <code>pending_payment</code>), paid or cancelled, and a defined next step when a payment attempt is interrupted or declined. We will return to that boundary after storing and querying the order itself.</p>

<h2 id="tables-describe-facts-and-relationships">Tables, keys and relationships</h2>
<p>A <strong>table</strong> holds rows with named, typed <strong>columns</strong>. Each <strong>row</strong> represents one instance of a fact: one customer, one order or one product within an order. Say what that instance means before choosing its columns. Here are the shop’s four tables, with the rows the next two sections use. Arun’s order 102 is a draft: he has started it but added nothing yet.</p>
<ShopTables />
<p>A <strong>primary key</strong> uniquely identifies a row and cannot be missing. Two customers can share a name, and Mina can change hers, so the shop gives each customer an integer identifier. Order 101 stores Mina’s identifier, 1, rather than her name. Correcting her name then changes one customer row rather than a copy on every order.</p>
<p>The order’s <code>customer_id</code> is a required <strong>foreign key</strong>: it must identify an existing customer. An order for customer 999 is refused if no such customer exists. A customer may still have no orders. Each item similarly references an order and a product. Its primary key is <code>(order_id, product_id)</code>, a pair, because this shop combines the same product into one quantity per order. If the shop recorded a discount as a separate item, it would need a different primary key.</p>
<p>The <strong>schema</strong> defines these tables, types and rules. Quantity is an integer, required with <code>NOT NULL</code> and positive with <code>CHECK (quantity &gt; 0)</code>. Rules the database enforces like these, keys included, are called <strong>constraints</strong>. The price is pounds to two decimal places, stored as the exact decimal type <code>numeric(10,2)</code>. It records the purchased price: tomorrow’s catalogue change must not rewrite yesterday’s agreement.</p>
<p><code>NULL</code> is SQL’s marker for a missing value. An order’s <code>shipped_at</code> is NULL until shipment is recorded, as on both orders above. It is neither a date nor the text “NULL”. If the shop must distinguish “not shipped” from “shipment time unknown”, it needs to represent that distinction too.</p>
<p>These are choices about what the facts mean. How the engine lays rows out on disk, and the extra lookup structures called indexes that help it find them, are separate choices, explained in <a href="#correct-sql-still-has-to-do-physical-work">The work behind a query</a>. Adding an index can make an order cheaper to find without changing what it means. Keeping customer and order facts in separate tables also leaves them available together through a join, which we will use next.</p>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/data-design/">Designing relational data</a> builds and tests a complete schema from requirements, including the rules it cannot enforce. Shorter follow-up articles cover values, constraints, normalisation and history.</p>

<h2 id="ask-for-a-result-then-check-what-it-means">Asking questions with SQL</h2>
<p><strong>SQL</strong> is the language most relational systems use to define, read and change data. A <code>SELECT</code> asks for rows. This one asks for Mina’s order identifiers and statuses:</p>
<Sql code={selectOrders} label="Mina’s orders, in identifier order" />
<p><code>FROM</code> names the input table, <code>WHERE</code> keeps rows meeting the condition and <code>SELECT</code> chooses the output columns. In the <code>orders</code> table above, only order 101 has <code>customer_id</code> 1, so the result is one row:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Result of the query for Mina’s orders"><table><caption>Result</caption><thead><tr><th>order_id</th><th>status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>101</td><td>paid</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><code>ORDER BY</code> requests the result order. Without <code>ORDER BY</code>, the rows can come back in any sequence, even if they look sorted today.</p>
<p>A <strong>join</strong> pairs rows with matching values. Join each order to its customer and items, then use <code>GROUP BY</code> to gather the matches for each order. <code>SUM</code> adds quantity times purchased price:</p>
<Sql code={summary} label="An item total for each order with items" />
<p>The short names <code>o</code>, <code>c</code> and <code>i</code> are table aliases. Figure 1 has already joined each order to its customer, so the names are shown. Predict how many rows order 101 will produce when it meets its two items, then step through. At the last step, choose <em>Keep: LEFT JOIN</em> and compare Arun’s order.</p>
<RowsFigure />
<p>Order 101 appeared twice, once per item, before grouping made one £36 total. Counting those joined rows would count item matches, not orders. Both rows are legitimate: each represents a different order and item pair. Decide what one output row should mean before trying to remove repeats.</p>
<p>The ordinary join omitted order 102 because it had no item. A <code>LEFT JOIN</code> keeps an unmatched order with NULLs on the item side. Its sum is NULL because there are no item values to add. Displaying zero is a separate decision. <code>COUNT(*)</code> would count the retained row as one; <code>COUNT(i.product_id)</code> counts non-null product values and gives zero.</p>
<p>Missing values affect filters too. <code>shipped_at = NULL</code> produces an unknown comparison, not a missing-value test. Use <code>shipped_at IS NULL</code>. A <code>WHERE</code> keeps only true conditions, so the wrong test silently excludes the rows you wanted.</p>
<p>SQL describes the requested result; the engine chooses how to find it. A query that runs and returns plausible rows can still be answering the wrong question. Changes carry the same risk: an <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code> or <code>DELETE</code> can succeed and still make the wrong change, as the next section shows with the last mug. Check an expected answer on a tiny dataset, including empty orders and repeated matches, before trusting it on the shop’s data.</p>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/sql/">Working with SQL</a> supplies a disposable practice database and builds one sales report from its first query to an answer you have checked, followed by pagination and changes.</p>

<h2 id="a-transaction-protects-a-change-within-a-stated-contract">Transactions and competing buyers</h2>
<p>A transaction is how the shop makes the three changes for a new order, 103, succeed or fail together. The application sends <code>BEGIN</code>, then the statements, then <code>COMMIT</code>. In PostgreSQL, the transaction sees its own unfinished changes; other <strong>sessions</strong> (other connections to the database) do not. <code>COMMIT</code> keeps the changes together. <code>ROLLBACK</code> discards them together.</p>
<p>Play Figure 2 with separate commits and a failed request. At the last step, choose <em>One transaction</em> and compare what remains. Then choose <em>Completes</em>, reset and watch when another session sees the stock fall.</p>
<OrderFigure />
<p>Separate commits left order 103 and its item with the mug still available: promised and for sale at once. Rolling back one transaction removed both new rows. On success, commit made all three changes visible together. This all-or-nothing property is <strong>atomicity</strong>. Here are the order, item and reservation steps of order 103’s transaction:</p>
<Sql code={checkout} label="Order 103: order, item and reservation" />
<p><code>SET</code> gives a column its new value, here calculated from the old one. The <code>WHERE</code> clause limits the change to the mug, and only while one is available. <code>RETURNING</code> sends back the changed row’s new value, so the application learns whether it reserved a mug. If one row comes back, the application sends <code>COMMIT</code>. If no row comes back, none was left, and it sends <code>ROLLBACK</code> so the order and item disappear too.</p>
<p>Now Mina and Arun both try to buy the last mug. Suppose each application first reads the stock, then writes the value it calculated. Each can see that one mug is available before either commits. Each transaction is atomic, and the combined result can still be wrong. <strong>Isolation</strong> defines which interactions between overlapping transactions the engine permits. Figure 3 shows both approaches; switch to the conditional <code>UPDATE</code> from the listing and compare the outcome.</p>
<RaceFigure />
<p>Reading, then writing, accepted two orders for one mug, and every statement succeeded. The conditional <code>UPDATE</code> keeps the check and the change in one statement. When Mina’s <code>UPDATE</code> changes the mug row, her transaction takes a <strong>lock</strong> on that row: a claim that stops other transactions changing it until hers commits or rolls back. Arun’s <code>UPDATE</code> waits for that lock.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL offers several isolation levels; its default is called <em>Read Committed</em>. At that level, once Mina’s transaction ends, Arun’s <code>UPDATE</code> tests <code>available &gt;= 1</code> again against the new value, 0, and changes nothing. His application must treat the missing row as no reservation and roll back his order.</p>
<p>Defaults differ between engines: MySQL’s InnoDB starts at Repeatable Read, for example, so check the chosen engine’s documented behaviour for this statement before relying on it. The schema’s <code>CHECK (available &gt;= 0)</code> constraint adds a second guard, but the application must still check whether the <code>UPDATE</code> returned a row.</p>
<p>That handles a rule about one row. A rule that spans several rows needs a design that covers every program changing them, using constraints, locks or a stricter isolation level. <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Making concurrent changes correct</a> develops those choices and tests competing sessions. The initials <strong>ACID</strong> collect atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability. Consistency here means preserving the rules the system has been given. ACID cannot add rules nobody wrote down, or undo a mistaken change.</p>
<p><strong>Durability</strong> is the promise that a committed change survives a crash. Before PostgreSQL replies that a commit succeeded, it normally waits until a record of the change, in its write-ahead log, has been saved to disk, so the change can be rebuilt after a crash. A setting called asynchronous commit replies before that save, and a crash can then lose the last few commits. Neither protects against losing the disk itself. Before relying on a success reply, check which engine and settings you have and which failures they cover.</p>
<p>The payment provider remains outside this transaction. The same transaction that inserts order 103 as <code>pending_payment</code> and reserves the mug also inserts a stored request to take payment, with its own payment-request identifier. A <strong>background worker</strong>, a separate process that runs outside any customer’s request, then asks the provider. If the provider declines, one transaction marks order 103 cancelled and returns the mug to stock. If the provider’s reply is lost, the worker asks the provider about that payment-request identifier before trying again.</p>

<h2 id="the-application-is-part-of-the-database-system">From a request to a committed order</h2>
<p>Every statement reaches the database through the application’s code, and several of the shop’s worst failures happen there: values mistaken for SQL, transactions split across connections and retries that create duplicate orders. A <strong>driver</strong> is the library that sends the application’s statements and converts the values that come back. For a database service, it uses a network connection to the server; an embedded engine runs inside the application process instead.</p>
<p>Supply Mina’s identifier as a bound value. PostgreSQL statement text can say <code>WHERE customer_id = $1</code>, with the integer passed separately through the driver’s binding API. Her input then remains a value rather than becoming SQL syntax. The application must also establish that the signed-in user may read customer 1’s orders. Binding an identifier does not authorise it.</p>
<p>A server application usually borrows a connection from a bounded <strong>pool</strong>. Its transaction must stay on that connection until commit or rollback; sending <code>BEGIN</code> on one connection and the updates on another does not group them. Finish and clean up the transaction before returning the connection, and keep unrelated network calls outside it. An open transaction keeps its locks until it ends, so one checkout stalled inside a transaction can block every later buyer of the mug. A server-side limit on idle open transactions, such as PostgreSQL’s <code>idle_in_<wbr>transaction_<wbr>session_<wbr>timeout</code>, bounds how long an abandoned one can do that.</p>
<p>A constraint error means the change did not happen. A connection that breaks during <code>COMMIT</code> tells you nothing: the order may have been saved without the application hearing. So give each purchase attempt its own identifier, such as <code>R103</code>, separate from the customer and order identifiers. The shop adds a <code>purchase_attempt</code> column to <code>orders</code> with a unique constraint, stores R103 there in the same transaction as the order, and reuses it for every retry of that purchase. Step through Figure 4, then switch where the connection breaks and compare the two paths.</p>
<DecisionFigure scene="outcome" number={4} />
<p>The same connection error accompanied a committed order and an uncommitted one. Retrying with a new purchase-attempt identifier could have created a second order; retrying with R103 could not. If the first attempt is still open when the retry arrives, the retry’s insert waits for it to end, and the unique constraint lets only one of them commit. A lookup that finds no order for R103 is therefore not enough on its own: the constraint is what stops a second order.</p>
<p>Give the payment request a stable identifier too, and reuse it for retries where the provider supports duplicate prevention. When a payment’s outcome is unknown, ask the provider what happened before acting again. A timeout limits how long the caller waits; it does not prove the remote work stopped. Limit how many times and how quickly requests retry, and how many connections they may open, because retrying at once makes an overloaded database worse. <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">Building applications around a relational database</a> follows the complete request, including value conversion, connection cleanup and recoverable work outside the transaction. One of its follow-ups, <a href="/review/relational-next/connections/">Managing connections and transaction lifetimes</a>, sets the pool and idle-transaction limits.</p>

<h2 id="correct-sql-still-has-to-do-physical-work">The work behind a query</h2>
<p>Finding Mina’s orders can mean testing every row in the <code>orders</code> table. An <strong>index</strong> gives the engine a searchable route from a value to the rows that hold it. A primary key usually comes with one, so finding order 101 by <code>order_id</code> is already quick. Finding a customer’s orders by <code>customer_id</code> is not, unless an index on <code>orders.customer_id</code> exists.</p>
<p>Engines store several rows together in blocks called <strong>pages</strong>. Reading one row means reading its whole page. Figure 5 uses twelve invented orders, stored in the order they arrived, to keep both routes visible. Step through the scan, then switch to <em>Searching an index</em>. Compare the table pages read, as well as the orders returned.</p>
<DecisionFigure scene="lookup" number={5} />
<p>The index saved reading two of the four pages here; on a large table with few matches, it can save most of the table. A request for nearly every order may reach most pages anyway and be cheaper as a scan. Each index also takes space and adds work to every change that must update it. Joins, sorting and sending large results consume resources even when the final answer contains few rows.</p>
<p>A <strong>query plan</strong> shows the route the engine chose. PostgreSQL’s <code>EXPLAIN</code> reports its estimates; <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> also runs the statement and adds the rows and pages it actually touched. Because it really runs the statement, an <code>UPDATE</code> or <code>DELETE</code> really changes data: measure those on a throwaway copy.</p>
<p>For a slow order-history page, record its SQL, parameters, data size and delay. Find where the time goes: waiting for a connection, executing or transferring the answer. If execution dominates, inspect the measured plan. If the plan reads thousands of rows to return ten, look at how it finds them. If it spends its time waiting for a lock, the problem is elsewhere. Predict what one change should remove, compare the same correct result before and after, then check writes and concurrent requests too.</p>
<p>Requests share CPU, memory, storage, locks and connections. Averages can hide the slowest responses, so compare latency percentiles and completed requests per second under realistic competing work. <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Understanding and improving database performance</a> completes that diagnosis and comparison. Its evidence then leads to <a href="/review/relational-next/query-plans/">query-plan diagnosis</a> for expensive execution or <a href="/review/relational-next/concurrent-load/">Improving performance under concurrent load</a> for queues and contention.</p>

<h2 id="data-and-applications-change-at-different-speeds">Changing a live database</h2>
<p>The shop now wants a delivery instruction with every order. Existing orders have none stored in the database, and older application instances never send one. Filling every old row with an empty string may satisfy a rule while hiding that the information was never collected.</p>
<p>In Figure 6, compare requiring the value as soon as the column is added with waiting until every application version sends it and old rows are filled. Follow what happens to the old application’s next order.</p>
<DecisionFigure scene="change" number={6} />
<p>Add the column as optional first. Deploy a version that supplies the value, convert existing rows only where a real value can be found, such as the courier records in Figure 6, then check the rows and retire the old version before requiring the value. If some history has no valid value, the final rule must allow those historical orders. A deployment is not finished while an old instance, worker or scheduled job can still write the old format.</p>
<p>The database has physical work to do as well: a schema change may scan or rewrite rows and hold locks. Even a quick change, such as adding an optional column, usually needs a lock that blocks all other use of the table. While it waits for that lock, ordinary requests queue behind it. Give the change a short lock-wait limit, and retry if the limit is reached. Rehearse on representative data with realistic concurrent work, allow spare capacity and give a long conversion bounded batches and recorded progress. Decide how to stop or resume it. Reverting code or recreating a dropped column does not bring back lost values.</p>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">Changing a live database</a> carries one representation change through compatibility, conversion, verification and retirement, then extends the method to moving a database. One of its follow-ups, <a href="/review/relational-next/schema-changes/">Changing schemas without breaking applications</a>, sets lock-wait limits for PostgreSQL.</p>

<h2 id="operate-for-the-failure-you-must-recover-from">Running and recovering the database</h2>
<p>The shop needs the database to stay usable and its answers to stay trustworthy. Give ordinary requests only the access they need, protect credentials and network access, and name the people responsible for upkeep and recovery. Watch errors and latency alongside connections, waits and storage growth. Leave capacity for a surge, an index build or a recovery, as well as today’s traffic.</p>
<p>Ordinary writes also create upkeep. In PostgreSQL, an update or delete leaves the old version of the row in place for a while, so that transactions already running can still read it. Routine cleanup, <code>VACUUM</code>, later reclaims that space. The engine also keeps statistics about the data to choose query plans, and <code>ANALYZE</code> refreshes them; PostgreSQL’s autovacuum normally runs both. A transaction left open stops cleanup removing versions it might still need. When cleanup falls behind, for that or any other reason, tables grow and plans get slower.</p>
<p>So far the shop has one database server, the <strong>primary</strong>, which accepts every change. A <strong>replica</strong> is another server that receives a copy of each change. If the primary fails, a replica can be promoted to take its place; that switch is called <strong>failover</strong>. Whether acknowledged orders survive it depends on how far the replica had caught up and what each commit waited for, and reads from a replica may lag. A <strong>backup</strong> is a copy of the data from an earlier moment, stored separately. With the change history kept since the backup, PostgreSQL can recover to a chosen point, such as just before a mistake.</p>
<p>Figure 7 starts with order 101 on the primary, the replica and a backup. Compare losing the primary with committing a deletion, and watch which copy could recover the order.</p>
<DecisionFigure scene="recovery" number={7} />
<p>Replication copied the mistaken deletion. A backup answers that failure, but returning the whole database to that earlier moment would also lose valid later orders. Restore it separately, check the affected relationships and decide how to reconcile them with current work before changing the live service.</p>
<p>State acceptable loss and interruption for each failure. The allowed loss is the <strong>recovery point objective</strong>; the allowed recovery duration is the <strong>recovery time objective</strong>. A successful backup job does not establish either. Time an isolated restore and application checks, including the encryption keys, credentials, configuration and capacity needed to run it. For the shop, check orders with their items, stock reservations and payment-request identifiers. A running server with wrong orders has not finished recovering.</p>
<p>A managed service takes on specified duties; the team still needs to check its retention and restore limits, its remaining responsibilities and recovery access. <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Running a relational service</a> connects those requirements to access, monitoring, maintenance and incident decisions. One of its follow-ups, <a href="/review/relational-next/maintenance-and-upgrades/">Maintaining and upgrading a relational service</a>, makes upkeep routine.</p>

<h2 id="choose-a-system-by-testing-the-contract-you-need">Choosing a database and a reading route</h2>
<p>The shop has related facts, integrity rules, changes that belong together and questions that will evolve. Those make a relational system a useful candidate. Specialised search or analytical work may justify another store, with additional synchronisation and recovery costs. Begin with the work and promises rather than the product name.</p>
<p>An embedded library such as SQLite runs with the application. A server such as PostgreSQL accepts client connections. A distributed relational service coordinates data across machines. Shared SQL does not give them identical write concurrency, transaction behaviour or recovery. The engine, its arrangement of copies and who operates it are separate choices.</p>
<p>Write a short selection brief: the stock rule, peak competing orders, response-time target, data growth, acceptable loss and interruption, and the team’s operating capacity. Turn hard requirements into rejection tests. Exercise candidates with the same schema and competing work through the intended driver and network, and include recovery and operating cost. <a href="/review/relational-next/choosing/">Choosing a relational database</a> develops that evaluation and keeps a PostgreSQL candidate record. Take its <a href="/review/relational-next/sqlite/">SQLite follow-up</a> for an embedded candidate, or its <a href="/review/relational-next/cockroachdb/">CockroachDB follow-up</a> when a concrete distributed requirement survives comparison with a simpler service.</p>
<p>You can begin learning before that choice is complete. Use the disposable PostgreSQL practice database at the start of <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/">Working with SQL</a>, then choose the route matching your job.</p>
