<p>Read a longer article’s worked example before its shorter follow-ups. If you start partway through the collection for a particular task, each article says which earlier sections it relies on. A newcomer can then build the whole picture in order, while an experienced reader can go straight to the question that matters now.</p>

<h2 id="sources">Sources and further reading</h2>
<p>Primary documentation checked on 19 and 20 October 2026. The shop and all seven figures are illustrative models; they do not contact a database. Separately, we ran the shop’s rows, the SQL results, the order 103 listing and the outcomes shown in Figures 2, 3, 4 and 6 on a PostgreSQL 18.6 server; each gave the result the article shows. The transaction examples assume ordinary logged tables and PostgreSQL’s Read Committed behaviour. They are not a complete checkout or payment implementation.</p>
<ul>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-altertable.html">ALTER TABLE</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/storage-page-layout.html">Database page layout</a>: existing values, locks and validation when strengthening a rule, and rows stored in pages.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-constraints.html">Constraints</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/datatype-numeric.html">Numeric types</a>: keys, references, unique and required values, and exact decimal types.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/queries-table-expressions.html">Table expressions</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-aggregate.html">Aggregate functions</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-comparison.html">Comparison functions</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/queries-order.html">Sorting rows</a>: joins, grouping, missing values and result ordering.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/tutorial-transactions.html">Transactions</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/transaction-iso.html">Transaction isolation</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-async-commit.html">Asynchronous commit</a>: all-or-nothing changes, concurrent updates and the durability setting. MySQL 8.4, <a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/innodb-transaction-isolation-levels.html">InnoDB transaction isolation levels</a>: InnoDB’s default level.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/libpq-exec.html#LIBPQ-PQEXECPARAMS">Parameterised command execution</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/libpq-status.html">Connection status functions</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/protocol-flow.html">Message flow</a>: separating values from command text, session termination and the unknown transaction status of a bad connection. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-client.html">Client connection defaults</a>: <code>idle_in_<wbr>transaction_<wbr>session_<wbr>timeout</code> and <code>lock_timeout</code>, both off by default.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-intro.html">Introduction to indexes</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/using-explain.html">Using EXPLAIN</a>: access paths, their maintenance costs and proposed work versus measured execution. SQLite, <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/serverless.html">Serverless</a>: an engine running within the application process.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/routine-vacuuming.html">Routine vacuuming</a>: reclaiming old row versions and refreshing planner statistics. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/continuous-archiving.html">Continuous archiving and point-in-time recovery</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/warm-standby.html">Log-shipping standby servers</a>: backups with retained change history, replication and failover boundaries.</li>
</ul>
